-/goal {"kind":"Stage Goal Context","issue_identifier":"#35","title":"maxConcurrentAgents for each stage","description":"## Recommended PRD refinement\n\nUse **Stage Concurrency Policy** as the canonical framing. The feature should add per-Stage Agent limits without replacing the existing global `agent.maxConcurrentAgents` cap.\n\n### Recommended decisions\n\n- Keep `agent.maxConcurrentAgents` as the global maximum number of running agents. A Stage Concurrency Policy can allocate that capacity by Stage Agent, but it must never let total running agents exceed the global cap.\n- Define the per-stage limit on each `stageAgents.stages[]` entry, not on raw project status names. One Stage Agent can own multiple statuses such as `Todo`, `To-Do`, and `In progress`; that whole entry should share one concurrency limit.\n- Use current running issue state to compute available slots per Stage Agent on each poll. Do not pre-spawn idle agents. If a stage has one issue available and a limit of two, launch one agent.\n- When an agent finishes, the next poll should refill only the stage slots that have dispatchable issues.\n- Preserve Ordered Queue semantics: the queue controls admission order, and stage caps only limit whether an otherwise eligible queued issue has capacity to run.\n- For this Self-Dogfooding Workspace Repository, keep practical concurrency at one until safe parallel Task Branch Integration is implemented by #36. After #36, a recommended dogfood shape is planner `1`, engineer `2`, reviewer `2`, with global `agent.maxConcurrentAgents` set high enough to permit the intended total.\n\n### Acceptance criteria to add\n\n- Runtime Settings can configure per-Stage Agent concurrency limits while preserving the current global cap.\n- Scheduler dispatches across multiple stages in one poll when each stage has capacity.\n- Scheduler does not exceed either the stage cap or the global cap.\n- Stages with no dispatchable issue do not launch idle agents.\n- Retry and blocked/Human Attention tasks still count according to existing running/retrying semantics and are not double-dispatched.\n- Documentation explains that stage caps are capacity limits, not a request to keep agents permanently alive.\n\n### Tests to require\n\n- Config parsing for omitted per-stage limit, valid positive limit, and invalid zero/negative limit.\n- Dispatch with Backlog limit `1`, engineer limit `2`, reviewer limit `2`, and global cap high enough to run all available stage slots.\n- A single issue in a stage with limit `2` launches only one agent.\n- Global cap lower than the sum of stage caps still limits total running agents.\n- Ordered Queue plus Stage Concurrency Policy preserves queue admission order.\n- No duplicate dispatch for issues already running, retrying, blocked, or in Human Attention Status.\n\n---\ntitle: Stage Concurrency Policy\nversion: 1.0\ndate_created: 2026-05-06\nlast_updated: 2026-05-06\nowner: Product Repository maintainers\ntags: [process, runtime-settings, scheduler, stage-agents, issue-35]\n---\n\n# Introduction\n\nThis specification defines Stage Concurrency Policy for Personal Symphony. The goal is to let a Workspace Repository configure how many agents may run for each Stage Agent while preserving the global `agent.maxConcurrentAgents` cap.\n\nSource issue: [#35 maxConcurrentAgents for each stage](https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/35).\n\n## 1. Purpose & Scope\n\nThis specification applies to Runtime Settings, scheduler capacity calculation, Ordered Queue admission, dispatch behavior, Runtime State diagnostics, and tests for concurrent Stage Agent execution.\n\nThe intended audience is implementers and reviewers working on scheduling and stage dispatch behavior.\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- Keeping idle agents alive when no issue is dispatchable.\n- Replacing the global `agent.maxConcurrentAgents` cap.\n- Increasing concurrency for this Self-Dogfooding Workspace Repository before safe parallel Task Branch Integration is implemented.\n\nAny implementation that changes runtime semantics MUST add or update an ADR under `docs/adr/`.\n\n## 2. Definitions\n\n- **Runtime Settings**: The `settings.json` portion of the Runtime Contract.\n- **Stage Agent**: A configured stage entry that owns one or more project statuses and transition behavior.\n- **Stage Concurrency Policy**: A Runtime Settings rule that limits how many agents may run for a specific Stage Agent.\n- **Ordered Queue**: A CLI-provided sequence of issue identifiers used as the dispatch order for eligible work.\n- **Human Attention Status**: A paused project status for task work that requires operator triage.\n- **Task Branch**: A Git branch created from the Loop-Start Branch for one dispatched task.\n- **Task Branch Integration**: The act of bringing completed Task Branch commits into the Loop-Start Branch.\n\n## 3. Requirements, Constraints & Guidelines\n\n- **REQ-001**: Runtime Settings MUST support a Stage Concurrency Policy on each `stageAgents.stages[]` entry.\n- **REQ-002**: Stage Concurrency Policy MUST apply to the Stage Agent entry, not to raw project status names.\n- **REQ-003**: One Stage Agent that owns multiple statuses MUST share one concurrency limit across all owned statuses.\n- **REQ-004**: The global `agent.maxConcurrentAgents` value MUST remain the maximum total number of running agents.\n- **REQ-005**: Symphony MUST NOT exceed either a Stage Agent concurrency limit or the global concurrency cap.\n- **REQ-006**: Omitted per-stage concurrency MUST preserve current behavior for that Stage Agent.\n- **REQ-007**: A configured per-stage concurrency limit MUST be a positive integer.\n- **REQ-008**: Invalid zero, negative, non-integer, or non-numeric limits MUST be Runtime Settings validation errors.\n- **REQ-009**: Scheduler capacity MUST be recomputed from current running issue state on each poll.\n- **REQ-010**: Symphony MUST NOT pre-spawn idle agents.\n- **REQ-011**: If one issue is dispatchable for a Stage Agent with limit `2`, Symphony MUST launch only one agent.\n- **REQ-012**: When an agent finishes, a later scheduler poll MUST refill only stage slots that have dispatchable issues.\n- **REQ-013**: Ordered Queue admission order MUST remain authoritative for queued work.\n- **REQ-014**: Stage Concurrency Policy MUST only decide whether an otherwise eligible issue has capacity to run.\n- **REQ-015**: Running, retrying, blocked, and Human Attention tasks MUST NOT be double-dispatched.\n- **REQ-016**: Runtime State SHOULD expose enough data to explain per-stage running counts and blocked capacity.\n- **CON-001**: This Self-Dogfooding Workspace Repository SHOULD keep practical concurrency at one until safe parallel Task Branch Integration is implemented.\n- **GUD-001**: After safe parallel Task Branch Integration exists, a recommended dogfood shape is planner `1`, engineer `2`, reviewer `2`, with a global cap high enough to permit the intended total.\n\n## 4. Interfaces & Data Contracts\n\n### Runtime Settings Shape\n\nThe exact field name may be adjusted during implementation, but per-stage capacity MUST be configured on the Stage Agent entry.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"agent\": {\n    \"maxConcurrentAgents\": 5\n  },\n  \"stageAgents\": {\n    \"stages\": [\n      {\n        \"agent\": \"planner\",\n        \"statuses\": [\"Backlog\"],\n        \"successStatus\": \"To-Do\",\n        \"concurrency\": 1\n      },\n      {\n        \"agent\": \"engineer\",\n        \"statuses\": [\"To-Do\", \"In progress\"],\n        \"successStatus\": \"In review\",\n        \"concurrency\": 2\n      },\n      {\n        \"agent\": \"reviewer\",\n        \"statuses\": [\"In review\"],\n        \"successStatus\": \"Done\",\n        \"concurrency\": 2\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Scheduler Capacity Contract\n\n| Input | Expected result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Stage limit has available slot and global cap has available slot | Dispatch next eligible issue for that Stage Agent. |\n| Stage limit is full | Do not dispatch more issues for that Stage Agent. |\n| Global cap is full | Do not dispatch any additional issue. |\n| Stage has no dispatchable issue | Launch no agent for that Stage Agent. |\n| Ordered Queue has eligible entry but stage cap is full | Keep entry pending until capacity exists. |\n\n## 5. Acceptance Criteria\n\n- **AC-001**: Given Backlog limit `1`, engineer limit `2`, reviewer limit `2`, and global cap `5`, When all stages have dispatchable issues, Then Symphony can dispatch up to five agents across those Stage Agents.\n- **AC-002**: Given engineer limit `2` and one eligible engineer issue, When the scheduler polls, Then Symphony launches one engineer agent.\n- **AC-003**: Given stage limits sum to `5` and global cap is `3`, When five issues are eligible, Then Symphony runs no more than three agents total.\n- **AC-004**: Given an Ordered Queue, When the next queued issue has stage capacity, Then it is admitted according to queue order.\n- **AC-005**: Given an Ordered Queue entry is otherwise eligible but its Stage Agent is at capacity, When a later queue entry has different stage capacity, Then queue semantics determine whether the later entry may be admitted.\n- **AC-006**: Given an issue is already running, retrying, blocked, or in Human Attention Status, When the scheduler polls, Then that issue is not double-dispatched.\n\n## 6. Test Automation Strategy\n\n- **Test Levels**: Unit and backend integration tests.\n- **Frameworks**: OCaml Alcotest.\n- **Test Data Management**: Synthetic issue lists, Stage Agent fixtures, Ordered Queue fixtures, and temporary Runtime Settings files.\n- **CI/CD Integration**: Run `pnpm test`.\n- **Coverage Requirements**: Cover parsing, capacity calculation, global cap interaction, queue behavior, and duplicate dispatch prevention.\n- **Performance Testing**: Not required for first version.\n\nRequired tests:\n\n- Config parsing for omitted per-stage limit, valid positive limit, invalid zero limit, and invalid negative limit.\n- Dispatch with Backlog limit `1`, engineer limit `2`, reviewer limit `2`, and a global cap high enough to run all available stage slots.\n- Single issue in a stage with limit `2` launches only one agent.\n- Global cap lower than the sum of stage caps still limits total running agents.\n- Ordered Queue plus Stage Concurrency Policy preserves queue admission order.\n- No duplicate dispatch for issues already running, retrying, blocked, or in Human Attention Status.\n\n## 7. Rationale & Context\n\nThe current global concurrency cap is not expressive enough for workflows where different stages should have different throughput. A planner stage may need one agent, while implementation and review stages may safely use more than one.\n\nThe policy belongs on Stage Agent entries because a Stage Agent may own multiple project statuses. Limiting raw statuses would split capacity incorrectly and make scheduler behavior harder to reason about.\n\nSafe concurrency also depends on Task Branch Integration. Until unrelated concurrent Task Branches can integrate without avoidable merge attention, this Self-Dogfooding Workspace Repository should keep operational concurrency low.\n\n## 8. Dependencies & External Integrations\n\n### External Systems\n\n- **EXT-001**: GitHub Issues + Projects - Provides dispatchable issue states.\n\n### Third-Party Services\n\n- **SVC-001**: Codex agent runtime - Receives launched agents for eligible issues.\n\n### Infrastructure Dependencies\n\n- **INF-001**: Runtime Home - Stores Runtime Settings and Runtime State.\n\n### Data Dependencies\n\n- **DAT-001**: Issue state snapshot - Determines dispatchable, running, retrying, blocked, and Human Attention work.\n- **DAT-002**: Ordered Queue state - Determines admission order when an Ordered Queue is active.\n\n### Technology Platform Dependencies\n\n- **PLT-001**: OCaml backend scheduler - Computes stage and global capacity.\n\n### Compliance Dependencies\n\n- **COM-001**: ADR coverage - Runtime semantic changes require an ADR.\n\n## 9. Examples & Edge Cases\n\n```json\n{\n  \"globalCap\": 3,\n  \"stageCaps\": {\n    \"planner\": 1,\n    \"engineer\": 2,\n    \"reviewer\": 2\n  },\n  \"running\": {\n    \"planner\": 1,\n    \"engineer\": 1,\n    \"reviewer\": 0\n  },\n  \"availableGlobalSlots\": 1,\n  \"availableStageSlots\": {\n    \"planner\": 0,\n    \"engineer\": 1,\n    \"reviewer\": 2\n  }\n}\n```\n\nEdge cases:\n\n- A Stage Agent owns `Todo`, `To-Do`, and `In progress`: count all running issues for those statuses against one Stage Agent limit.\n- A stage limit exceeds the global cap: allow the configuration but the global cap still controls total running agents.\n- No dispatchable issue exists for a stage with open capacity: launch no idle agent.\n\n## 10. Validation Criteria\n\n- `pnpm test` passes after implementation.\n- Scheduler never exceeds stage or global caps.\n- Runtime Settings validation rejects invalid limits.\n- Ordered Queue behavior remains deterministic.\n- Runtime State provides enough information to explain why capacity did or did not dispatch work.\n\n## 11. Related Specifications / Further Reading\n\n- [CONTEXT.md](../CONTEXT.md)\n- [Issue #35](https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/35)\n- [ADR directory](../docs/adr/)\n- [Task Branch Integration specification](./spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md)\n","comments":[],"url":"https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/35","current_project_status":"In Progress","labels":["enhancement"],"priority":null,"blocker_references":[],"attempt":0,"stage_agent_name":"engineer"}
+/goal {"kind":"Stage Goal Context","issue_identifier":"#37","title":"Accept Agent Context Snapshot Runtime Semantics","description":"## Specification\n\nSpec: `spec/spec-architecture-agent-context-snapshot-runtime-semantics.md`\nADR: `docs/adr/0019-agent-context-snapshot-runtime-semantics.md`\nGlossary: `CONTEXT.md`\n\n## Accepted model\n\nAgent Context Snapshot and Context Command are Workspace Repository scoped, harness-level behaviors. Symphony generates bounded launch context in its own launch harness and must not install or mutate global Codex lifecycle hooks.\n\nOwnership boundaries:\n\n- Runtime Settings own operator-configurable Agent Context Snapshot and Context Command behavior.\n- Agent Prompt composition owns prompt injection.\n- Runtime State may expose live context generation status and bounded diagnostics references.\n- Ignored Runtime Diagnostics may store bounded, secret-free context metadata.\n\nPrompt semantics:\n\n- Agent Context Snapshot supplements the Agent Prompt.\n- Agent Context Snapshot supplements Stage Goal Handoff when both are enabled.\n- Agent Context Snapshot must not replace the Agent Prompt or Stage Goal Handoff.\n- Context Command stdout may supplement Agent Context Snapshot content.\n- Context Command stderr and failures remain diagnostic by default.\n\nConstraints:\n\n- Preserve existing Runtime Contract defaults unless the operator explicitly approves a default change.\n- Do not persist `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `GH_TOKEN`, local `.env` contents, full rendered Agent Prompts, or full Codex transcripts in Runtime Contract files.\n- Use precise terms such as Agent Context Snapshot and Context Command; avoid ambiguous phrases such as \"full context\" and \"context compression\".\n\n## Documentation completed\n\n- [x] Added ADR under `docs/adr/` stating context generation happens in the Symphony launch harness, not through Codex lifecycle hooks.\n- [x] Defined which parts belong in Runtime Settings, Runtime State, Agent Prompt composition, and ignored Runtime Diagnostics.\n- [x] Stated that Agent Context Snapshot supplements the Agent Prompt and Stage Goal Handoff.\n- [x] Stated that Context Command stdout may supplement the Agent Prompt, while stderr and failures remain diagnostic by default.\n- [x] Updated `CONTEXT.md` with accepted terms and avoid-list wording for ambiguous phrases such as \"full context\" or \"context compression\".\n- [x] Preserved existing Runtime Contract defaults unless the operator explicitly approves a default change.\n\n## Blocked by\n\nNone - ready for implementation slices.\n\n---\ntitle: Agent Context Snapshot Runtime Semantics\nversion: 1.0\ndate_created: 2026-05-06\nlast_updated: 2026-05-06\nowner: Product Repository maintainers\ntags: [architecture, agent-context, runtime-contract, issue-37]\n---\n\n# Introduction\n\nThis specification defines the accepted runtime semantics for Agent Context Snapshot and Context Command behavior in Personal Symphony.\n\nSource issue: [#37 Accept Agent Context Snapshot Runtime Semantics](https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/37).\n\n## 1. Purpose & Scope\n\nThe purpose is to define domain language and architecture boundaries before implementation changes Runtime Settings, Runtime State, Agent Prompt composition, or Runtime Home diagnostics.\n\nThis specification applies to the Product Repository implementation and to Workspace Repository Runtime Contract behavior.\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- Implementing Context Command execution.\n- Implementing independent stage completion review.\n- Installing or mutating global Codex lifecycle hooks.\n\n## 2. Definitions\n\n- **Workspace Repository**: The repository where the operator runs Personal Symphony.\n- **Product Repository**: The Personal Symphony source repository.\n- **Runtime Home**: The `.symphony/` directory in a Workspace Repository.\n- **Runtime Contract**: Repository-owned Runtime Home files that define Personal Symphony behavior.\n- **Runtime Settings**: The `.symphony/settings.json` portion of the Runtime Contract.\n- **Runtime State**: Ignored Runtime Home files and backend snapshots that record current orchestration activity.\n- **Agent Prompt**: The rendered task instruction sent to an agent.\n- **Stage Goal Handoff**: A stage-specific Codex handoff that sets a Codex goal before the normal Agent Prompt.\n- **Agent Context Snapshot**: A bounded, deterministic context section generated by the Symphony launch harness before agent launch or retry.\n- **Context Command**: An optional operator-configured local command whose bounded stdout may supplement Agent Context Snapshot content.\n\n## 3. Requirements, Constraints & Guidelines\n\n- **REQ-001**: Agent Context Snapshot MUST be generated by the Symphony launch harness.\n- **REQ-002**: Agent Context Snapshot MUST be scoped to the active Workspace Repository.\n- **REQ-003**: Agent Context Snapshot MUST supplement, not replace, the Agent Prompt.\n- **REQ-004**: Agent Context Snapshot MUST coexist with Stage Goal Handoff.\n- **REQ-005**: Runtime Settings MUST own operator-configurable context behavior.\n- **REQ-006**: Runtime State MAY expose context generation status and bounded diagnostics.\n- **REQ-007**: Ignored Runtime Home diagnostics MAY store bounded metadata about context generation.\n- **REQ-008**: `CONTEXT.md` MUST define accepted context terminology.\n- **CON-001**: The implementation MUST NOT install or mutate global Codex lifecycle hooks.\n- **CON-002**: The implementation MUST NOT store full Codex transcripts in the Runtime Contract.\n- **CON-003**: The implementation MUST NOT persist `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `GH_TOKEN`, or local `.env` contents.\n- **GUD-001**: Use precise names such as Agent Context Snapshot and Context Command instead of ambiguous phrases such as \"full context.\"\n\n## 4. Interfaces & Data Contracts\n\n### Runtime Ownership\n\n| Concern | Owner | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Enablement and limits | Runtime Settings | User-editable Runtime Contract. |\n| Prompt injection | Agent Prompt composition | Generated immediately before launch. |\n| Live status | Runtime State | Exposed to CLI/server/dashboard snapshots. |\n| Debug metadata | Ignored Runtime Home diagnostics | Bounded and secret-free. |\n| Domain terms | `CONTEXT.md` | Source of truth for glossary. |\n\n### ADR Requirements\n\nAny accepted runtime semantic change MUST be documented in `docs/adr/`.\n\n## 5. Acceptance Criteria\n\n- **AC-001**: Given implementation begins, When runtime context semantics are introduced, Then an ADR exists under `docs/adr/`.\n- **AC-002**: Given `CONTEXT.md` is reviewed, When Agent Context Snapshot and Context Command are used, Then the terms are defined there.\n- **AC-003**: Given Stage Goal Handoff is enabled, When Agent Context Snapshot is enabled, Then both mechanisms remain distinct and ordered.\n- **AC-004**: Given Runtime Contract files are inspected, When diagnostics are generated, Then full prompts and secret values are absent.\n\n## 6. Test Automation Strategy\n\n- **Test Levels**: Documentation validation and backend tests in implementation slices.\n- **Frameworks**: OCaml Alcotest for backend behavior.\n- **Test Data Management**: Use temporary Workspace Repository fixtures.\n- **CI/CD Integration**: Run `pnpm test` after implementation slices.\n- **Coverage Requirements**: Each runtime semantic implemented later must have focused tests.\n- **Performance Testing**: Not required for this design slice.\n\n## 7. Rationale & Context\n\n`codex-loop` uses Codex lifecycle hooks for continuation. Symphony already owns agent launch, Agent Worktrees, prompt writing, retries, and Runtime State. Therefore, the transferable design is harness-level bounded context generation, not global hook installation.\n\n## 8. Dependencies & External Integrations\n\n### External Systems\n\n- **EXT-001**: GitHub Issues + Projects - Supplies issue metadata and stage state.\n- **EXT-002**: Git - Supplies branch and worktree state.\n\n### Infrastructure Dependencies\n\n- **INF-001**: Runtime Home - Stores Runtime Contract, Runtime State, and diagnostics.\n\n### Technology Platform Dependencies\n\n- **PLT-001**: OCaml backend - Owns settings parsing, prompt composition, and orchestration.\n\n### Compliance Dependencies\n\n- **COM-001**: ADR coverage - Required for runtime semantic changes.\n\n## 9. Examples & Edge Cases\n\n```text\nAgent Prompt\n---\nStage Goal Handoff content, when enabled\n---\nAgent Context Snapshot, when enabled\n```\n\nEdge cases:\n\n- Missing context settings means context behavior remains disabled.\n- Context diagnostics must not make Runtime Contract files non-idempotent.\n- Ambiguous domain terms must be rejected during documentation review.\n\n## 10. Validation Criteria\n\n- ADR exists and describes runtime ownership boundaries.\n- `CONTEXT.md` includes accepted terms and avoid-list wording.\n- Later implementation issues can reference this specification without redefining context semantics.\n\n## 11. Related Specifications / Further Reading\n\n- [Issue #37](https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/37)\n- [CONTEXT.md](../CONTEXT.md)\n- [Codex Loop Context Management Analysis](../docs/agent-context/codex-loop-context-management.md)\n- [Stage Goal Handoff ADR](../docs/adr/0007-stage-goal-handoff.md)\n","comments":[{"author":"MatheusBBarni","body":"---\ntitle: Agent Context Snapshot Runtime Semantics\nversion: 1.0\ndate_created: 2026-05-06\nlast_updated: 2026-05-06\nowner: Product Repository maintainers\ntags: [architecture, agent-context, runtime-contract, issue-37]\n---\n\n# Introduction\n\nThis specification defines the accepted runtime semantics for Agent Context Snapshot and Context Command behavior in Personal Symphony.\n\nSource issue: [#37 Accept Agent Context Snapshot Runtime Semantics](https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/37).\n\n## 1. Purpose & Scope\n\nThe purpose is to define domain language and architecture boundaries before implementation changes Runtime Settings, Runtime State, Agent Prompt composition, or Runtime Home diagnostics.\n\nThis specification applies to the Product Repository implementation and to Workspace Repository Runtime Contract behavior.\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- Implementing Context Command execution.\n- Implementing independent stage completion review.\n- Installing or mutating global Codex lifecycle hooks.\n\n## 2. Definitions\n\n- **Workspace Repository**: The repository where the operator runs Personal Symphony.\n- **Product Repository**: The Personal Symphony source repository.\n- **Runtime Home**: The `.symphony/` directory in a Workspace Repository.\n- **Runtime Contract**: Repository-owned Runtime Home files that define Personal Symphony behavior.\n- **Runtime Settings**: The `.symphony/settings.json` portion of the Runtime Contract.\n- **Runtime State**: Ignored Runtime Home files and backend snapshots that record current orchestration activity.\n- **Agent Prompt**: The rendered task instruction sent to an agent.\n- **Stage Goal Handoff**: A stage-specific Codex handoff that sets a Codex goal before the normal Agent Prompt.\n- **Agent Context Snapshot**: A bounded, deterministic context section generated by the Symphony launch harness before agent launch or retry.\n- **Context Command**: An optional operator-configured local command whose bounded stdout may supplement Agent Context Snapshot content.\n\n## 3. Requirements, Constraints & Guidelines\n\n- **REQ-001**: Agent Context Snapshot MUST be generated by the Symphony launch harness.\n- **REQ-002**: Agent Context Snapshot MUST be scoped to the active Workspace Repository.\n- **REQ-003**: Agent Context Snapshot MUST supplement, not replace, the Agent Prompt.\n- **REQ-004**: Agent Context Snapshot MUST coexist with Stage Goal Handoff.\n- **REQ-005**: Runtime Settings MUST own operator-configurable context behavior.\n- **REQ-006**: Runtime State MAY expose context generation status and bounded diagnostics.\n- **REQ-007**: Ignored Runtime Home diagnostics MAY store bounded metadata about context generation.\n- **REQ-008**: `CONTEXT.md` MUST define accepted context terminology.\n- **CON-001**: The implementation MUST NOT install or mutate global Codex lifecycle hooks.\n- **CON-002**: The implementation MUST NOT store full Codex transcripts in the Runtime Contract.\n- **CON-003**: The implementation MUST NOT persist `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `GH_TOKEN`, or local `.env` contents.\n- **GUD-001**: Use precise names such as Agent Context Snapshot and Context Command instead of ambiguous phrases such as \"full context.\"\n\n## 4. Interfaces & Data Contracts\n\n### Runtime Ownership\n\n| Concern | Owner | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Enablement and limits | Runtime Settings | User-editable Runtime Contract. |\n| Prompt injection | Agent Prompt composition | Generated immediately before launch. |\n| Live status | Runtime State | Exposed to CLI/server/dashboard snapshots. |\n| Debug metadata | Ignored Runtime Home diagnostics | Bounded and secret-free. |\n| Domain terms | `CONTEXT.md` | Source of truth for glossary. |\n\n### ADR Requirements\n\nAny accepted runtime semantic change MUST be documented in `docs/adr/`.\n\n## 5. Acceptance Criteria\n\n- **AC-001**: Given implementation begins, When runtime context semantics are introduced, Then an ADR exists under `docs/adr/`.\n- **AC-002**: Given `CONTEXT.md` is reviewed, When Agent Context Snapshot and Context Command are used, Then the terms are defined there.\n- **AC-003**: Given Stage Goal Handoff is enabled, When Agent Context Snapshot is enabled, Then both mechanisms remain distinct and ordered.\n- **AC-004**: Given Runtime Contract files are inspected, When diagnostics are generated, Then full prompts and secret values are absent.\n\n## 6. Test Automation Strategy\n\n- **Test Levels**: Documentation validation and backend tests in implementation slices.\n- **Frameworks**: OCaml Alcotest for backend behavior.\n- **Test Data Management**: Use temporary Workspace Repository fixtures.\n- **CI/CD Integration**: Run `pnpm test` after implementation slices.\n- **Coverage Requirements**: Each runtime semantic implemented later must have focused tests.\n- **Performance Testing**: Not required for this design slice.\n\n## 7. Rationale & Context\n\n`codex-loop` uses Codex lifecycle hooks for continuation. Symphony already owns agent launch, Agent Worktrees, prompt writing, retries, and Runtime State. Therefore, the transferable design is harness-level bounded context generation, not global hook installation.\n\n## 8. Dependencies & External Integrations\n\n### External Systems\n\n- **EXT-001**: GitHub Issues + Projects - Supplies issue metadata and stage state.\n- **EXT-002**: Git - Supplies branch and worktree state.\n\n### Infrastructure Dependencies\n\n- **INF-001**: Runtime Home - Stores Runtime Contract, Runtime State, and diagnostics.\n\n### Technology Platform Dependencies\n\n- **PLT-001**: OCaml backend - Owns settings parsing, prompt composition, and orchestration.\n\n### Compliance Dependencies\n\n- **COM-001**: ADR coverage - Required for runtime semantic changes.\n\n## 9. Examples & Edge Cases\n\n```text\nAgent Prompt\n---\nStage Goal Handoff content, when enabled\n---\nAgent Context Snapshot, when enabled\n```\n\nEdge cases:\n\n- Missing context settings means context behavior remains disabled.\n- Context diagnostics must not make Runtime Contract files non-idempotent.\n- Ambiguous domain terms must be rejected during documentation review.\n\n## 10. Validation Criteria\n\n- ADR exists and describes runtime ownership boundaries.\n- `CONTEXT.md` includes accepted terms and avoid-list wording.\n- Later implementation issues can reference this specification without redefining context semantics.\n\n## 11. Related Specifications / Further Reading\n\n- [Issue #37](https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/37)\n- [CONTEXT.md](../CONTEXT.md)\n- [Codex Loop Context Management Analysis](../docs/agent-context/codex-loop-context-management.md)\n- [Stage Goal Handoff ADR](../docs/adr/0007-stage-goal-handoff.md)\n","created_at":"2026-05-06T03:24:48Z","url":"https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/37#issuecomment-4384841067"}],"url":"https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/37","current_project_status":"In Progress","labels":["enhancement","post alpha"],"priority":null,"blocker_references":[],"attempt":2,"stage_agent_name":"engineer"}
 
